  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Promoting Spaces for Conflict Prevention and Reduced Vulnerability in Abyei: A Joint Programme of the RCs in Sudan and South Sudan in Abyei (hereafter the UN Joint Programme in Abyei)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The rationale behind the UN Joint Programme is that vulnerability and severe poverty and gender discrimination reinforce fault lines and act as conflict drivers between communities in the Abyei area. Competition over natural resources, especially during migration season, is an additional conflict trigger and often increases the risk of reprisal attacks and vicious cycles of violence including targeted attacks on women and children. The severe effects of climate change and the lack of interventions proposing mitigating these effects for the agro-pastoralist communities in the Abyei box further increase community vulnerabilities and compromise efforts to promote peaceful coexistence. 
 
While it is beyond the scope of this Joint UN Programme to address the overarching question of the final status of Abyei, it is designed to address vulnerabilities through increased access to services. Based on the various assessments carried out, the programme posits that these are immediate priorities for violence reduction and community stabilization, including enabling of inter-communal dialogues, while filling the gaps in service provision as incentives for continued peace. An underlying assumption is that increased stability could lead to an improved environment for political dialogue and peaceful problem-solving strategies between South Sudan and Sudan in the contested area.</narrative>
  </description>
